# The committed template for .env, which is not committed. To start:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# .gitignore ignores .env and .env.* and then re-includes .env.example and
# .env.*.example by negation. That negation is the only reason this file
# reaches the repository, so keep the .example suffix exactly: a file named
# .env.local or .env.sample is silently ignored and never arrives.
#
# Every variable the application reads belongs here with a placeholder value.
# One that lives only in a local .env is invisible to everyone else, and the
# first symptom is a fresh clone failing at startup on a key nobody knew to
# set.
#
# Placeholders only, never a working value. gitleaks runs at commit and
# catches recognizable credential shapes, not an arbitrary string that
# happens to be live -- it is a backstop, not the check.
#
# Conventions: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE, no spaces around '=', quotes only when the
# value contains spaces or '#'. This is not a shell script. Whether $OTHER
# expands, and whether a trailing '# comment' after a value is stripped or
# swallowed into it, both depend on the loader -- put comments on their own
# line above the variable and neither question comes up.
#
# Note above each variable whether it is required and what values it accepts.
# Nothing else records that, and a required variable reads exactly like an
# optional one that happens to be set.
#
# Shape to follow:
#
# LOG_LEVEL=info
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/dbname
# SESSION_SECRET=replace-me-generate-with-openssl-rand-hex-32
#
# Per-environment files follow the same rule: .env.production.example is
# committed and .env.production is not.
